  "sanity_passed": true,
  "author_support": "proved_here",
  "justification": "For each Pocklington witness and each prime r dividing n, the order of the base modulo r divides n-1 and contains the full displayed q-power. Thus F divides r-1. F squared greater than n rules out any prime divisor at most sqrt(n). For an elliptic witness the discriminant is a unit and every division in the group operations uses a unit. The finite point Q reduces modulo every prime divisor r to a point of certified prime order q. There are at most 2r+1 points, including infinity, since each x has at most two y values. The checked size of q rules out r at most sqrt(n). A certified row also requires the exact product identity."
 }
